  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Indiana has received $3.7B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Award activity totals 18,070 awards with an average award size of $205K, and obligations fell 47.0% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in the state, obligating $2.5B in FY2026 to date. ROLLS-ROYCE CORPORATION is the largest vendor, receiving $874M in obligations. Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing is the largest industry by obligations at $794M. Other sizable buying agencies include the Department of Homeland Security with $321M, the Department of Health and Human Services with $288M, and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $183M. Other top vendors include NATIONAL GOVERNMENT SERVICES, INC. with $266M and LMI CONSULTING, LLC with $252M. Engineering Services accounts for $322M in obligations, and Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services accounts for $260M. Fiscal-year history shows obligations ranging from $8.2B in FY22 to $3.7B in FY26, reflecting large swings in annual spending levels.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal buying in Indiana?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $2.5B of the state's $3.7B in FY2026 to date. The top three agencies together include the Department of Homeland Security with $321M and the Department of Health and Human Services with $288M, which concentrates a large share of obligations among a few agencies. The top vendor, ROLLS-ROYCE CORPORATION, received $874M, indicating vendor concentration in key industries like aircraft engines."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors matter most for Indiana contractors?",
        "body": "Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing leads industries with $794M in obligations, followed by Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $335M. Major vendors include ROLLS-ROYCE CORPORATION at $874M, NATIONAL GOVERNMENT SERVICES, INC. at $266M, and LMI CONSULTING, LLC at $252M, showing both manufacturing and professional services play significant roles. Engineering Services and Administrative Management consulting together account for $322M and $260M respectively."
      }
    ]
  },
